  "query_preview": "/MONDAY THROUGH SATURDAY/...\nIssued at 1234 AM MDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nModels and ensembles continue to be in good agreement, all\ntrending drier through the extended period. What was once an\nactive looking pattern is become less so with each model cycle.\n\nMonday...the upper high will remain stretched across Colorado as\nthe upper trough in the Pacific Northwest moves into the\nNorthern Rockies. Weak energy moving through the ridge will lead\nto isolated to scattered thunderstorms across the higher\nterrain. The main threats from convection will be lightning and\ngusty outflow winds. Drier air will keep precipitation rates\nlow, with most evaporating before reaching the ground. Heat will\ncontinue to be the main concern. Temperatures will continue warm\nby about 3-5 degrees F Monday compared to Sunday. Highs on the\nPlains will top out from near 100 to around 107. Heat Advisories\nlook good, for El Paso, Pueblo and eastern Fremont Counties, the\nlower Arkansas River Valley and the San Luis Valley. Highs could\nbe pushing daily records. Please continue to take precautions to\nprotect yourself and pets from the heat.\n\nAs the upper trough moves into Montana, the upper ridge will be\nforced south, with increasing westerly flow across Colorado\nMonday night. This will push convection off the higher terrain\nacross the Plains during the evening and overnight hours.\nLightning and gusty outflow winds may accompany storms. Expect\nthunderstorms to exit into Kansas by Tuesday morning. A cold\nfront will be arrive late Monday night on the Plains, with a\nnortheasterly wind shift.\n\nTuesday and Wednesday...the upper trough will slowly move east\nacross the Northern Plains into the Upper Midwest for mid week.\nThis will keep westerly flow across Colorado, with the upper\nhigh pushed to the south. What once looked like an active period\nhas really dried out. PWAT values look to remain under 1 inch,\nand energy moving through the westerly flow continues to weaken\nwith each run. The cold front on Tuesday will switch winds\nnortheasterly, with upslope flow developing showers and\nthunderstorms along the Eastern Mountains Tuesday afternoon.\nPrecipitation rates continue to decrease with ever lower PWAT\nvalues, and flash flooding on area burn scars is not as likely\nas it may have once been. But, we will still monitor in case a\nheavier storm moves over Aspen Acres. Very similar conditions\nare forecast for Wednesday, with another round of afternoon\nshowers and thunderstorms, mainly across the higher terrain.\nConvection looks to initiate over the Mountains and drift\nsoutheast by the evening before dissipating across the Plains.\nTemperatures will be cooler with 80s and lower 90s both days.\n\nThursday into Sunday...models continue to converge on a drier\nsolution. The ECMWF has been consistently building the high\npressure over the Desert Southwest north across Colorado. The\nGFS and Canadian along with the ensembles are all starting to\nlatch onto this solution. While weak embedded energy may develop\ndaily afternoon and evening thunderstorms across the higher\nterrain, coverage is more sparse than in previous runs.\nTemperatures will also be on the rebound for late in the week,\nwith 90s to near 100 by next weekend.",
